GP-0: Add GhidrAssist extension (Claude-powered RE
 assistant)

Introduces Ghidra/Extensions/GhidrAssist, a new extension that embeds a
Claude-powered assistant inside Ghidra:

- Docked chat panel with streaming responses.
- Decompiler right-click actions: Explain Function, Suggest Renames
  (confirmed and applied in a single transaction), Suggest Signature,
  Reconstruct as C.
- Tools menu action to export the current program as a buildable-ish C
  project skeleton (per-function .c files, program.h, Makefile, README).

The Anthropic API key, model, and base URL are configured via
Edit -> Tool Options -> GhidrAssist. The extension depends on :Base and
:Decompiler and ships a standard distributable zip via :zipExtensions.

Known limits documented in the extension's README and the exported
project's README: decompilation is lossy; rename and type suggestions are
hypotheses; and the exported C project is a starting point for manual
porting, not a byte-exact rebuild pipeline.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 <noreply@anthropic.com>

GP-0: GhidrAssist Apple-aware context + Prepare Apple Binary action

Adds Apple-binary awareness to the GhidrAssist extension. No new
decompilation capability -- Ghidra already handles Mach-O, dyld shared
caches, AARCH64:LE:64:AppleSilicon, and ships the Objective-C and Swift
feature modules. This change wires the LLM assistant into those
capabilities more usefully.

- apple/AppleBinaryContext detects Mach-O, Apple Silicon, Objective-C
  runtime (via __objc_* sections), Swift runtime (via __swift5_*
  sections), and arm64e hints from the executable path. Pure-string
  classifier is exposed as AppleBinaryContext.classify(...) for tests.
- Explain and Reconstruct actions prepend a short Apple preamble when
  the current program is Apple-flavoured: objc_msgSend dispatch, Swift
  mangling and metadata sections, Darwin AAPCS64 conventions, and
  pointer authentication on arm64e.
- New Tools -> GhidrAssist -> Prepare Apple Binary... action enables
  the Objective-C Message / Type Metadata and Swift Type Metadata
  analyzers on the current program and schedules re-analysis, inside a
  single transaction so Undo rolls everything back.
- Unit tests under src/test cover ELF vs Mach-O, Apple Silicon vs
  x86_64 Mach-O, Objective-C and Swift section detection, arm64e path
  hints, null safety, and preamble content. All 10 tests pass.

End-to-end verification on a real Mach-O binary requires a macOS /
iOS target and a running Ghidra install, which were not available in
the development environment. Compilation (:GhidrAssist:compileJava),
packaging (:GhidrAssist:zipExtensions), and unit tests
(:GhidrAssist:test) all succeed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 <noreply@anthropic.com>
